  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is resonance in engineering?",
      "a": "Resonance happens when a system is pushed at its own natural frequency. Each push adds energy in step with the motion, so the vibration grows until damping balances it.",
      "asks": [
        "what causes resonance"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the amplitude ratio graph show?",
      "a": "It shows how many times bigger the steady vibration is than the deflection a slow, steady force of the same size would cause, plotted against the ratio of forcing frequency to natural frequency.",
      "asks": [
        "magnification factor curve",
        "frequency response curve"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does damping reduce resonance?",
      "a": "Damping takes energy out every cycle. At resonance the amplitude ratio is one over 2 zeta, so more damping gives a lower peak.",
      "asks": [
        "effect of damping on resonance"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is vibration isolation?",
      "a": "Mounting a machine on soft springs so that its running frequency is more than the square root of 2 times the natural frequency. Then less force reaches the floor than the machine produces.",
      "asks": [
        "how to isolate vibration"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a washing machine shake at one speed?",
      "a": "Clothes bunched on one side make the drum unbalanced. As the spin speed rises, it passes through the natural frequency of the drum on its springs, and the machine shakes hard for a moment.",
      "asks": [
        "washing machine resonance"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "At resonance, the steady amplitude of a damped spring-mass system is 1 over 2 zeta times the static deflection.",
    "Vibration isolation needs the forcing frequency to be more than the square root of 2 times the natural frequency."
  ],
